======= Full description and examples=====
Ok so for the Air shipments (import/export) here are the fields which are the most important and which are usually filled:

1. DATE (this is just the current date)
2. OUR FILE NO. (ITS THE IDENTIFIER FOR THE SHIPMENT) in our case it is always or almost always in the email and starts with "HNXO". The same FILE NO should be written in the "INVOCING REFERENCE" field.
3. Pickup (Address where the trucker should pick up the goods from). If an phone number/ name for the pick up is provided then its a good idea to write it in this field. (But don't add any phone number/name it has to be related to the pickup location.
4. Delivery to (The Address to which the driver must deliver the goods to) same applies for phone number and name (if they are present for this field then add them but dont add a name/ phone number if you are not sure that its related to the delivery address
5. ROUTE This is usually just the Origin airport and destination airports in a format like this: Origin airPort--Destination airport
6. Bill to (this field is static it will always contain the same information so you dont even have to worry about it we will just write it in the form)
7. AWB NO. This is usually found in the pdf (in the air waybill)
8. Date of arrival this is the date on which the goods will be delivered to the destination port (This has a value only if its and import (meaning something came from somewhere to an airport and is waiting to be delivered to the destination address) and not an export)
9. Carrier its the company doing the sea/air transport (so its not the trucker) (e.g. Hapag-Lloyd) it is always in the somewhere in the pdf (often times you see their logo in the header or something else)
10. Origin port The port of origin (for import it can come from anywhere, and for export usually the Delivery to address is almost the the address as the address for the origin port)
11. Destination port the port of destination (usually when its import the pickup is the almost the same address as the port of destination itself, while for export the port of destination can be anywhere).

12. Mark (this is where you describe what is being transported usually a container or a pallet) (usually found in the pdf but email also
13. description of what is inside the pallet/container (usually "show equipment" or something along those lines) IMPORTANT NOTE : for a container you also always write the container no and the seal no
14. PKGS this is the number of packages in the pallet/container (Usually its written in the pdf) but if no information is available then the default is 1.
15. Gross weight this is usually written in the pdf but sometimes also in the email (in the pdf its usually called the same thing "gross weight").

Those 14 are the most important fields. All the other fields are left empty we don't care about them the only fields that we care are the 14 I mentioned above. If there is no information about any of these 14 fields then we just leave it empty.

Sometimes, the PDF may contain information for more then one container. How we handle those is simple, the number of container equals the number of D.O. that we need to make for this PDF. Everything stays the same when there is more then one container the only fields which change are the fields 12, 13, 14 (and often times 12 stays the same) what usually changes is only the 13 and 14 since the container no, seal no and weight will be different.

